{"task_id": "HumanEval/10", "solution": "def is_palindrome(string: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\" Test if given string is a palindrome \"\"\"\n    return string == string[::-1]\n\n\ndef make_palindrome(string: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\" Find the shortest palindrome that begins with a supplied string.\n    Find the longest suffix that is a palindrome and append the reversed prefix before it.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not string:\n        return \"\"\n\n    longest_pal_suffix_start_idx = len(string)\n    for i in range(len(string)):\n        suffix = string[i:]\n        if is_palindrome(suffix):\n            longest_pal_suffix_start_idx = i\n            break\n\n    prefix_to_mirror = string[:longest_pal_suffix_start_idx]\n    return string + prefix_to_mirror[::-1]"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/10", "solution": "def is_palindrome(string: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\" Test if given string is a palindrome \"\"\"\n    return string == string[::-1]\n\n\ndef make_palindrome(string: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\" Find the shortest palindrome that begins with a supplied string.\n    Append to the end of the string reverse of a string prefix that comes before the palindromic suffix.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(string)\n    # Find the starting index 'i' of the longest palindromic suffix.\n    # Initialize i_found to 0, which correctly handles the empty string case.\n    i_found = 0 \n    for i in range(n):\n        suffix = string[i:]\n        if is_palindrome(suffix):\n            i_found = i\n            break\n    \n    # The prefix that needs to be reversed and appended is string[:i_found].\n    prefix_to_reverse = string[:i_found]\n    return string + prefix_to_reverse[::-1]"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/11", "solution": "To implement the software design specification, the code will be structured into multiple files within the `src/bitxor` directory, along with a `pyproject.toml` for packaging.\n\nHere is the complete codebase:\n\n```python\n# src/bitxor/__init__.py\n\"\"\"\nbitxor - A Python library for binary XOR operations.\n\nProvides string_xor for bitwise XOR of two bit-strings,\nand a CLI for command-line usage with history persistence.\n\"\"\"\n\n__version__ = \"0.1.0\"\n\nfrom .string_ops import string_xor\nfrom .xor_service import XORService\nfrom .cli_controller import CLIController\nfrom .exceptions import InvalidBitStringError, LengthMismatchError, RepositoryError\nfrom .config import AppConfig\nfrom .history_repository import HistoryRepository\nfrom .validator import BitStringValidator\nfrom .cli import main # Expose main for console_scripts\n```\n\n```python\n# src/bitxor/bitstring.py\n\"\"\"\nDomain value object for a validated bit-string.\n\"\"\"\n\nclass BitString:\n    \"\"\"\n    Represents a validated bit-string, ensuring immutability.\n    \"\"\"\n    VALID_CHARS = frozenset({'0', '1'})\n\n    def __init__(self, value: str) -> None:\n        if not isinstance(value, str):\n            raise TypeError(\"BitString value must be a str\")\n        self._value = value\n\n    def value(self) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Returns the encapsulated string value.\"\"\"\n        return self._value\n\n    def __str__(self) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Returns the string representation, which is its value.\"\"\"\n        return self._value\n```\n\n```python\n# src/bitxor/string_ops.py\n\"\"\"\nCore functionality for performing bitwise XOR on bit-strings.\n\"\"\"\nfrom typing import List\nfrom .exceptions import InvalidBitStringError, LengthMismatchError\n\ndef string_xor(a: str, b: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the bitwise XOR of two equal-length bit-strings.\n    Inputs must be strings composed only of '0's and '1's.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(a, str) or not isinstance(b, str):\n        raise TypeError(\"Inputs must be strings\")\n    if len(a) != len(b):\n        raise LengthMismatchError(f\"Inputs must have equal length: {len(a)} != {len(b)}\")\n\n    # Validate characters (defensive check, validator also checks)\n    for ch in a + b:\n        if ch not in {'0', '1'}:\n            raise InvalidBitStringError(f\"Only '0' and '1' characters are allowed, got '{ch}'\")\n\n    return ''.join('1' if xa != xb else '0' for xa, xb in zip(a, b))\n\nclass StringOps:\n    \"\"\"\n    A utility class providing static methods for string operations,\n    primarily for encapsulating string_xor for potential future extensions or testing.\n    \"\"\"\n    @staticmethod\n    def string_xor(a: str, b: str) -> str:\n        \"\"\"\n        Static method wrapper for the module-level string_xor function.\n        Delegates to the canonical string_xor implementation.\n        \"\"\"\n        return string_xor(a, b)\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _xor_bits(a: str, b: str) -> List[str]:\n        \"\"\"Helper to produce list of result characters.\"\"\"\n        return ['1' if xa != xb else '0' for xa, xb in zip(a, b)]\n```\n\n```python\n# src/bitxor/validator.py\n\"\"\"\nInput validation for bit-strings.\n\"\"\"\nfrom .exceptions import InvalidBitStringError, LengthMismatchError\nfrom .bitstring import BitString # For VALID_CHARS\n\nclass BitStringValidator:\n    \"\"\"\n    Validates that input strings are valid bit-strings and checks length matching for pairs.\n    \"\"\"\n    def validate(self, value: str) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Validates a single string to ensure it's a bit-string.\n        Raises TypeError for non-str, InvalidBitStringError for invalid characters.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(value, str):\n            raise TypeError(\"Value must be a string.\")\n        for ch in value:\n            if ch not in BitString.VALID_CHARS:\n                raise InvalidBitStringError(f\"Invalid character '{ch}' found in bit-string.\")\n\n    def validate_pair(self, a: str, b: str) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Validates two strings as bit-strings and checks if their lengths match.\n        Raises InvalidBitStringError for invalid chars, LengthMismatchError for length disparity.\n        \"\"\"\n        self.validate(a)\n        self.validate(b)\n        if len(a) != len(b):\n            raise LengthMismatchError(f\"Bit-string lengths differ: '{a}' ({len(a)}) vs '{b}' ({len(b)})\")\n```\n\n```python\n# src/bitxor/xor_service.py\n\"\"\"\nService layer for orchestrating XOR computation and history persistence.\n\"\"\"\nimport uuid\nfrom datetime import datetime\nfrom .validator import BitStringValidator\nfrom .string_ops import string_xor\nfrom .history_repository import HistoryRepository\nfrom .config import AppConfig\nfrom .exceptions import RepositoryError\n\nclass XORService:\n    \"\"\"\n    Orchestrates the validation, computation, and optional persistence of XOR operations.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, validator: BitStringValidator, repo: HistoryRepository = None, config: AppConfig = None):\n        self._validator = validator\n        self._repo = repo\n        self._config = config\n\n    def compute(self, a: str, b: str, save_history: bool = False) -> str:\n        \"\"\"\n        Computes the XOR of two bit-strings, validating them first.\n        Optionally persists the operation history if a repository is configured.\n        \"\"\"\n        self._validator.validate_pair(a, b)\n        result = string_xor(a, b)\n\n        should_save = save_history or (self._config and self._config.default_save_history)\n        if should_save:\n            if not self._repo:\n                raise RepositoryError(\"History persistence is enabled but no repository is configured.\")\n            \n            entry = {\n                \"id\": str(uuid.uuid4()),\n                \"a\": a,\n                \"b\": b,\n                \"result\": result,\n                \"timestamp\": datetime.utcnow().isoformat(timespec='seconds') + \"Z\",\n                \"meta\": {\"source\": \"cli\" if save_history else \"service_default\"}\n            }\n            try:\n                self._repo.append(entry)\n            except Exception as e:\n                raise RepositoryError(f\"Failed to persist XOR operation history: {e}\") from e\n        return result\n```\n\n```python\n# src/bitxor/history_repository.py\n\"\"\"\nFile-backed repository for persisting XOR operation history to a JSON file.\n\"\"\"\nimport json\nimport os\nimport uuid\nfrom datetime import datetime\nfrom typing import List, Dict, Any\nfrom .exceptions import RepositoryError\n\nclass HistoryRepository:\n    \"\"\"\n    Manages the persistence of XOR operation history to a JSON file.\n    Ensures atomic writes and chronological appending.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, path: str):\n        self.path = os.path.expanduser(path)\n        # Ensure the directory for the history file exists\n        history_dir = os.path.dirname(self.path)\n        if history_dir: # Don't try to create a directory for current dir\n            os.makedirs(history_dir, exist_ok=True)\n\n    def append(self, entry: Dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Appends a new history entry to the JSON file.\n        Uses an atomic write strategy to prevent data corruption.\n        \"\"\"\n        try:\n            data: List[Dict[str, Any]] = []\n            if os.path.exists(self.path):\n                # Attempt to read existing data\n                with open(self.path, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n                    # Handle empty file case or invalid JSON gracefully\n                    file_content = f.read().strip()\n                    if file_content:\n                        data = json.loads(file_content)\n                    else:\n                        data = [] # Empty but valid file\n\n            data.append(entry)\n\n            # Write to a temporary file first, then replace the original\n            tmp_path = self.path + \".tmp\"\n            with open(tmp_path, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n                json.dump(data, f, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False) # indent for readability\n\n            # Atomically replace the old file with the new one\n            os.replace(tmp_path, self.path)\n\n        except (IOError, OSError, json.JSONDecodeError) as e:\n            raise RepositoryError(f\"Failed to append history to '{self.path}': {e}\") from e\n        except Exception as e:\n            # Catch other potential issues\n            raise RepositoryError(f\"An unexpected error occurred during history append: {e}\") from e\n\n    def list_all(self) -> List[Dict[str, Any]]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Retrieves all historical XOR operation entries.\n        Returns an empty list if the history file does not exist or is empty/invalid.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not os.path.exists(self.path):\n            return []\n        try:\n            with open(self.path, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n                file_content = f.read().strip()\n                if not file_content:\n                    return []\n                return json.loads(file_content)\n        except (IOError, OSError, json.JSONDecodeError) as e:\n            # Log the error if logging was implemented, then raise\n            raise RepositoryError(f\"Failed to read history from '{self.path}': {e}\") from e\n```\n\n```python\n# src/bitxor/cli_controller.py\n\"\"\"\nController responsible for handling command-line interface logic.\nIt processes CLI arguments, invokes the XORService, and manages output/exit codes.\n\"\"\"\nimport sys\nfrom .xor_service import XORService\nfrom .config import AppConfig\nfrom .exceptions import InvalidBitStringError, LengthMismatchError, RepositoryError\n\nclass CLIController:\n    \"\"\"\n    Manages the CLI interaction, bridging command-line arguments to service calls.\n    Handles error display and process exit codes.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, service: XORService, config: AppConfig):\n        self._service = service\n        self._config = config\n\n    def run(self, a: str, b: str, save_history: bool = False) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Executes the XOR computation based on CLI inputs.\n        Prints the result to stdout or an error message to stderr, and returns an exit code.\n        \"\"\"\n        try:\n            result = self._service.compute(a, b, save_history=save_history)\n            print(result)\n            return 0\n        except (InvalidBitStringError, LengthMismatchError) as e:\n            print(f\"Error: Invalid input provided. {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n            return 2 # Validation error exit code\n        except RepositoryError as e:\n            print(f\"Error: Failed to persist history. {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n            return 3 # Repository error exit code\n        except Exception as e:\n            print(f\"An unexpected error occurred: {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n            return 1 # General unexpected error\n```\n\n```python\n# src/bitxor/exceptions.py\n\"\"\"\nCustom exception classes for the bitxor library, providing specific error types.\n\"\"\"\n\nclass InvalidBitStringError(ValueError):\n    \"\"\"Raised when a bit-string contains invalid characters (not '0' or '1').\"\"\"\n    pass\n\nclass LengthMismatchError(ValueError):\n    \"\"\"Raised when two bit-strings have different lengths for an operation.\"\"\"\n    pass\n\nclass RepositoryError(RuntimeError):\n    \"\"\"Raised when an error occurs during history persistence operations.\"\"\"\n    pass\n```\n\n```python\n# src/bitxor/config.py\n\"\"\"\nApplication configuration for bitxor, including history path and persistence settings.\n\"\"\"\nimport os\nfrom typing import Optional\n\nclass AppConfig:\n    \"\"\"\n    Manages application configuration settings, such as history file path.\n    Can load settings from environment variables.\n    \"\"\"\n    DEFAULT_HISTORY_PATH: str = os.path.join(os.path.expanduser(\"~\"), \".bitxor\", \"history.json\")\n    DEFAULT_SAVE_HISTORY: bool = False\n\n    def __init__(self, history_path: str = DEFAULT_HISTORY_PATH, default_save_history: bool = DEFAULT_SAVE_HISTORY):\n        self.history_path = history_path\n        self.default_save_history = default_save_history\n\n    @classmethod\n    def from_env(cls) -> \"AppConfig\":\n        \"\"\"\n        Loads configuration settings, potentially overriding defaults with environment variables.\n        Environment variables: BITXOR_HISTORY_PATH, BITXOR_SAVE_HISTORY.\n        \"\"\"\n        history_path: Optional[str] = os.getenv(\"BITXOR_HISTORY_PATH\")\n        save_history_str: Optional[str] = os.getenv(\"BITXOR_SAVE_HISTORY\")\n\n        default_save_history: bool = cls.DEFAULT_SAVE_HISTORY\n        if save_history_str is not None:\n            # Interpret \"true\", \"1\", \"yes\" as True; anything else (including empty) as False\n            default_save_history = save_history_str.lower() in (\"true\", \"1\", \"yes\")\n\n        return cls(\n            history_path=history_path if history_path is not None else cls.DEFAULT_HISTORY_PATH,\n            default_save_history=default_save_history\n        )\n```\n\n```python\n# src/bitxor/cli.py\n\"\"\"\nCommand-line interface entry point for the bitxor tool.\nParses arguments, sets up dependencies, and invokes the CLIController.\n\"\"\"\nimport sys\nfrom typing import List\n\n# Import necessary components from the library\nfrom .config import AppConfig\nfrom .validator import BitStringValidator\nfrom .history_repository import HistoryRepository\nfrom .xor_service import XORService\nfrom .cli_controller import CLIController\n\ndef main(argv: Optional[List[str]] = None) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Main entry point for the bitxor-cli command.\n    Parses arguments, initializes the application, and runs the CLI controller.\n    \"\"\"\n    argv = argv if argv is not None else sys.argv[1:]\n\n    # Basic argument parsing\n    if not argv or len(argv) < 2 or \"--help\" in argv or \"-h\" in argv:\n        print(\"Usage: bitxor-cli <bitstring_a> <bitstring_b> [--save-history]\")\n        print(\"\\nComputes the bitwise XOR of two bit-strings.\")\n        print(\"  <bitstring_a> <bitstring_b> : Input bit-strings composed of '0's and '1's.\")\n        print(\"  --save-history              : Optional flag to save the operation to history.\")\n        return 1\n\n    a, b = argv[0], argv[1]\n    save_history_flag = \"--save-history\" in argv\n\n    # Initialize configuration\n    app_config = AppConfig.from_env()\n\n    # Initialize repository (only if a path is configured)\n    history_repo = None\n    if app_config.history_path:\n        try:\n            history_repo = HistoryRepository(app_config.history_path)\n        except Exception as e:\n            print(f\"Error: Could not initialize history repository at '{app_config.history_path}'. {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n            return 3 # Repository error\n\n    # Initialize validator and service\n    validator = BitStringValidator()\n    xor_service = XORService(validator=validator, repo=history_repo, config=app_config)\n\n    # Initialize and run CLI controller\n    controller = CLIController(service=xor_service, config=app_config)\n    return controller.run(a, b, save_history=save_history_flag)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    sys.exit(main())\n```\n\n```toml\n# pyproject.toml\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"setuptools>=61.0\"]\nbuild-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\"\n\n[project]\nname = \"bitxor\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"A Python library and CLI for binary XOR operations on bit-strings.\"\nreadme = \"README.md\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.8\"\nlicense = { text = \"MIT\" }\nkeywords = [\"xor\", \"binary\", \"bitwise\", \"bitstring\", \"cli\"]\nauthors = [\n  { name = \"Your Name\", email = \"your.email@example.com\" },\n]\nclassifiers = [\n    \"Development Status :: 3 - Alpha\",\n    \"Intended Audience :: Developers\",\n    \"License :: OSI Approved :: MIT License\",\n    \"Programming Language :: Python :: 3\",\n    \"Programming Language :: Python :: 3.8\",\n    \"Programming Language :: Python :: 3.9\",\n    \"Programming Language :: Python :: 3.10\",\n    \"Programming Language :: Python :: 3.11\",\n    \"Programming Language :: Python :: 3.12\",\n    \"Topic :: Utilities\",\n    \"Topic :: Software Development :: Libraries\",\n]\n\n[project.urls]\nHomepage = \"https://github.com/yourusername/bitxor\" # Replace with actual repo\n\"Bug Tracker\" = \"https://github.com/yourusername/bitxor/issues\" # Replace with actual repo\n\n[project.scripts]\nbitxor-cli = \"bitxor.cli:main\"\n\n[tool.setuptools.packages.find]\nwhere = [\"src\"]\n```"}
