{"task_id": "HumanEval/18", "solution": "def how_many_times(string: str, substring: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Find how many times a given substring can be found in the original string. Count overlapping cases.\n    The KMP algorithm is used for efficient substring searching.\n    \"\"\"\n    text, pattern = string, substring\n    n, m = len(text), len(pattern)\n\n    if m == 0:\n        return 0\n    if n == 0: # Handles empty string per docstring example.\n        return 0\n\n    # Build prefix function (pi table) for KMP algorithm.\n    pi = [0] * m\n    j = 0\n    for i in range(1, m):\n        while j > 0 and pattern[i] != pattern[j]:\n            j = pi[j-1]\n        if pattern[i] == pattern[j]:\n            j += 1\n        pi[i] = j\n\n    # Search for pattern in text using KMP.\n    count = 0\n    j = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        while j > 0 and text[i] != pattern[j]:\n            j = pi[j-1]\n        if text[i] == pattern[j]:\n            j += 1\n        if j == m:\n            count += 1\n            j = pi[j-1] # Move j back using pi table to allow for overlapping matches.\n    return count"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/18", "solution": "def how_many_times(string: str, substring: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Find how many times a given substring can be found in the original string. Counts overlapping cases.\n    The simplest logic is used, assuming valid inputs and allowing for empty substrings to match `len(string) + 1` times.\"\"\"\n    \n    n, m = len(string), len(substring)\n    count = 0\n    if m == 0:\n        # According to critical requirements, no exceptions for edge cases.\n        # Python's str.count('') returns len(string) + 1. Replicating this behavior\n        # for consistency with a 'simplest, most direct logic' interpretation when m=0.\n        return n + 1 \n    \n    for i in range(n - m + 1):\n        if string[i:i + m] == substring:\n            count += 1\n    return count"}
