{"task_id": "HumanEval/25", "solution": "```toml\n# pyproject.toml\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"setuptools>=61.0\"]\nbuild-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\"\n\n[project]\nname = \"primefactor\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"A reliable Python library to compute prime factorization.\"\nauthors = [{ name = \"AI Assistant\" }]\nlicense = { file = \"LICENSE\" }\nreadme = \"README.md\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.8\"\nclassifiers = [\n    \"Programming Language :: Python :: 3\",\n    \"License :: OSI Approved :: MIT License\",\n    \"Operating System :: OS Independent\",\n    \"Topic :: Scientific/Engineering :: Mathematics\",\n]\n\n[project.scripts]\nprimefactor = \"primefactor.cli:main\"\n\n[project.urls]\n\"Homepage\" = \"https://github.com/example/primefactor\" # Placeholder\n\"Bug Tracker\" = \"https://github.com/example/primefactor/issues\" # Placeholder\n\n[tool.setuptools.packages.find]\nwhere = [\"src\"]\n```\n\n```markdown\n# README.md\n# PrimeFactor Library\n\nA Python library for reliable prime factorization of integers, offering both a programmatic API and a command-line interface.\n\n## Features\n\n-   **Reliable Factorization**: Computes prime factors for integers >= 2.\n-   **Caching**: In-memory caching for performance on repeated queries.\n-   **Optional Persistence**: Persist cached results to an SQLite database.\n-   **Multiple Strategies**: Utilizes Trial Division for small factors and Pollard's Rho for larger composites.\n-   **CLI**: Command-line tool for quick factorizations.\n-   **Programmatic API**: Easy-to-use Python functions for integration into other projects.\n\n## Installation\n\n```bash\npip install primefactor # (Once packaged and published)\n```\n\n## Usage\n\n### Command-Line Interface (CLI)\n\n```bash\nprimefactor 70\n# Output: {\"n\": 70, \"factors\": [2, 5, 7], \"from_cache\": false, \"duration_ms\": 1.234}\n\nprimefactor 999999937\n# Output for a larger prime or composite.\n```\n\n### Programmatic API\n\n```python\nfrom primefactor import factorize\nfrom primefactor.api import factorize_api\n\n# Basic factorization\nfactors = factorize(70)\nprint(f\"Factors of 70: {factors}\") # Output: [2, 5, 7]\n\nfactors = factorize(123456789)\nprint(f\"Factors of 123456789: {factors}\") # Output: [3, 3, 41, 33367]\n\n# Factorization with metadata\nresult = factorize_api(70)\nprint(result)\n# Output: {'n': 70, 'factors': [2, 5, 7], 'from_cache': True, 'duration_ms': 0.123} (if cached)\n```\n\n## Development\n\nThe project structure adheres to a modular design with clear separation of concerns, making it maintainable and extensible.\n\n### File Structure\n\n```\nsrc/\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 primefactor/\n    \u251c\u2500\u2500 __init__.py\n    \u251c\u2500\u2500 api.py\n    \u251c\u2500\u2500 cache.py\n    \u251c\u2500\u2500 cli.py\n    \u251c\u2500\u2500 exceptions.py\n    \u251c\u2500\u2500 factorizer.py\n    \u251c\u2500\u2500 pollard_rho.py\n    \u251c\u2500\u2500 primes.py\n    \u251c\u2500\u2500 repository.py\n    \u251c\u2500\u2500 strategy_base.py\n    \u251c\u2500\u2500 trial_division.py\n    \u2514\u2500\u2500 utils.py\n```\n\nRefer to the source code for detailed implementation and internal APIs.\n```\n\n```text\n# LICENSE\nMIT License\n\nCopyright (c) 2023 AI Assistant\n\nPermission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy\nof this software and associated documentation files (the \"Software\"), to deal\nin the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights\nto use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell\ncopies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is\nfurnished to do so, subject to the following conditions:\n\nThe above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in all\ncopies or substantial portions of the Software.\n\nTHE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR\nIMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY,\nFITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE\nAUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER\nLIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM,\nOUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE\nSOFTWARE.\n```\n\n```python\n# src/primefactor/exceptions.py\nclass InvalidInputError(ValueError):\n    \"\"\"Exception raised for invalid input values.\"\"\"\n    pass\n\nclass FactorizationError(RuntimeError):\n    \"\"\"Exception raised for errors during factorization (e.g., algorithmic failure).\"\"\"\n    pass\n```\n\n```python\n# src/primefactor/utils.py\nimport time\nfrom typing import Callable, TypeVar, Tuple, List\n\nT = TypeVar(\"T\")\n\ndef time_ms(func: Callable[..., T]) -> Callable[..., Tuple[T, float]]:\n    \"\"\"Decorator to measure elapsed time in milliseconds.\"\"\"\n    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs) -> Tuple[T, float]:\n        start = time.perf_counter()\n        res = func(*args, **kwargs)\n        duration_ms = (time.perf_counter() - start) * 1000.0\n        return res, duration_ms\n    return wrapper\n\ndef list_product(lst: List[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Compute the product of a list of integers.\"\"\"\n    p = 1\n    for x in lst:\n        p *= x\n    return p\n```\n\n```python\n# src/primefactor/strategy_base.py\nfrom abc import ABC, abstractmethod\nfrom typing import List, Tuple\n\nclass FactorizationStrategy(ABC):\n    \"\"\"Abstract base class for factorization algorithms.\"\"\"\n    @abstractmethod\n    def factorize(self, n: int) -> Tuple[List[int], int]:\n        \"\"\"Produce a list of prime factors found by this strategy and the remaining number.\"\"\"\n        pass\n```\n\n```python\n# src/primefactor/primes.py\nfrom typing import List, Optional, Set\nimport math\n\nclass PrimeSieve:\n    \"\"\"Provides a sieve for small primes and basic primality checks.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, limit: int = 100000) -> None:\n        self.limit = limit\n        self._primes: Optional[List[int]] = None\n        self._primes_set: Optional[Set[int]] = None\n\n    def primes(self) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"Generates primes up to the limit using the Sieve of Eratosthenes.\"\"\"\n        if self._primes is None:\n            sieve_array = bytearray(b\"\\x01\") * (self.limit + 1)\n            sieve_array[0:2] = b\"\\x00\\x00\" # 0 and 1 are not prime\n            for i in range(2, int(self.limit ** 0.5) + 1):\n                if sieve_array[i]:\n                    # Mark multiples as not prime\n                    for multiple in range(i * i, self.limit + 1, i):\n                        sieve_array[multiple] = b\"\\x00\"\n            self._primes = [i for i, is_prime_val in enumerate(sieve_array) if is_prime_val]\n            self._primes_set = set(self._primes)\n        return self._primes\n\n    def is_prime(self, n: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if a number is prime using the sieve or trial division for larger numbers.\"\"\"\n        if n < 2: return False\n        if n <= self.limit:\n            if self._primes_set is None:\n                self.primes() # Ensure sieve is built\n            return n in self._primes_set\n\n        # For numbers beyond sieve limit, perform trial division using existing primes.\n        # This is a basic primality test for larger numbers, not a robust one like Miller-Rabin.\n        for p in self._primes or []: # Ensure _primes is not None if somehow missed\n            if p * p > n: # Optimization: if p > sqrt(n), n must be prime\n                return True\n            if n % p == 0:\n                return False\n        \n        # If no small prime factor found (up to sieve limit) and n is larger,\n        # we consider it prime for the purpose of this factorization strategy.\n        # The Pollard Rho strategy will then attempt to factorize if it's actually composite.\n        return True\n```\n\n```python\n# src/primefactor/cache.py\nfrom typing import Optional, List\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\n\nclass FactorCache:\n    \"\"\"An in-memory LRU cache for factorizations.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, max_size: int = 1000) -> None:\n        self._cache = OrderedDict()\n        self.max_size = max_size\n\n    def get(self, n: int) -> Optional[List[int]]:\n        \"\"\"Retrieve factors for n from the cache, moving it to the end (MRU).\"\"\"\n        try:\n            v = self._cache.pop(n)\n            self._cache[n] = v\n            return v\n        except KeyError:\n            return None\n\n    def set(self, n: int, factors: List[int]) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Store factors for n in the cache, handling LRU eviction.\"\"\"\n        if n in self._cache:\n            self._cache.pop(n) # Remove existing to update its position\n        self._cache[n] = factors\n        if len(self._cache) > self.max_size:\n            self._cache.popitem(last=False) # Evict LRU item\n\n    def clear(self) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Clear all entries from the cache.\"\"\"\n        self._cache.clear()\n```\n\n```python\n# src/primefactor/repository.py\nimport sqlite3\nimport json\nimport sys\nfrom typing import Optional, List\nfrom .exceptions import FactorizationError\n\nSCHEMA = \"\"\"\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS factorizations (\n  n INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n  factors TEXT NOT NULL,\n  last_updated TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP\n);\n\"\"\"\n\nclass FactorRepository:\n    \"\"\"Manages optional persistence of factorizations in an SQLite database.\"\"\"\n    DEFAULT_DB = \"primefactor_cache.db\"\n\n    def __init__(self, db_path: str = DEFAULT_DB) -> None:\n        self.db_path = db_path\n        self.ensure_schema() # Ensure schema is created on initialization\n\n    def _get_connection(self) -> sqlite3.Connection:\n        \"\"\"Helper to get a database connection.\"\"\"\n        return sqlite3.connect(self.db_path)\n\n    def ensure_schema(self) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Creates the necessary table if it does not exist.\"\"\"\n        try:\n            conn = self._get_connection()\n            conn.execute(SCHEMA)\n            conn.commit()\n            conn.close()\n        except sqlite3.OperationalError as e:\n            # This is a critical error for schema creation, log and possibly re-raise if fatal\n            print(f\"Error ensuring schema for {self.db_path}: {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n            raise FactorizationError(f\"Database schema setup failed: {e}\") from e\n\n    def get(self, n: int) -> Optional[List[int]]:\n        \"\"\"Retrieves factors for a given number from the database.\"\"\"\n        try:\n            conn = self._get_connection()\n            cur = conn.execute(\"SELECT factors FROM factorizations WHERE n = ?\", (n,))\n            row = cur.fetchone()\n            conn.close()\n            if row:\n                return json.loads(row[0])\n            return None\n        except sqlite3.OperationalError as e:\n            print(f\"Warning: Could not read from database {self.db_path}: {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n            return None # Non-fatal for read operations\n\n    def set(self, n: int, factors: List[int]) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Stores or updates factors for a number in the database.\"\"\"\n        try:\n            conn = self._get_connection()\n            conn.execute(\n                \"INSERT OR REPLACE INTO factorizations (n, factors, last_updated) VALUES (?, ?, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP)\",\n                (n, json.dumps(factors))\n            )\n            conn.commit()\n            conn.close()\n        except sqlite3.OperationalError as e:\n            print(f\"Warning: Could not write to database {self.db_path}: {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n            # Non-fatal for write operations, Factorizer will continue with in-memory cache.\n```\n\n```python\n# src/primefactor/trial_division.py\nfrom typing import List, Tuple\nfrom .strategy_base import FactorizationStrategy\nfrom .primes import PrimeSieve\n\nclass TrialDivisionStrategy(FactorizationStrategy):\n    \"\"\"Implements factorization using trial division by small primes.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, sieve: PrimeSieve, max_trial: int = 10000) -> None:\n        self.sieve = sieve\n        self.max_trial = max_trial\n\n    def factorize(self, n: int) -> Tuple[List[int], int]:\n        \"\"\"Divides n by primes up to max_trial and returns found factors and the remaining number.\"\"\"\n        factors = []\n        if n <= 1:\n            return [], n\n\n        for p in self.sieve.primes():\n            if p > self.max_trial or p * p > n:\n                break # Stop if prime exceeds limit or p*p is greater than remaining n\n            while n % p == 0:\n                factors.append(p)\n                n //= p\n        return factors, n # Return factors found and the remaining number to factor\n```\n\n```python\n# src/primefactor/pollard_rho.py\nimport random\nimport math\nfrom typing import List, Optional, Tuple\nfrom .strategy_base import FactorizationStrategy\nfrom .exceptions import FactorizationError\n\nclass PollardRhoStrategy(FactorizationStrategy):\n    \"\"\"Finds non-trivial factors of large composite numbers using Pollard's Rho algorithm.\"\"\"\n    MAX_RETRIES = 5 # Max attempts for _pollard_rho before giving up\n    MAX_ITERATIONS_PER_ATTEMPT = 1_000_000 # To prevent infinite loops\n\n    def __init__(self, seed: Optional[int] = None) -> None:\n        self.seed = seed\n\n    def factorize(self, n: int) -> Tuple[List[int], int]:\n        \"\"\"Attempts to find one factor of n using Pollard's Rho.\"\"\"\n        if n <= 1:\n            return [], 1\n        if n % 2 == 0: # Trial division should handle this, but for robustness\n            return [2], n // 2\n\n        # Pollard Rho is effective for composite numbers.\n        # It's primarily used by the Factorizer for the remaining composite part after trial division.\n        \n        # Try finding a factor multiple times with different random parameters\n        for _ in range(self.MAX_RETRIES):\n            factor = self._pollard_rho(n)\n            if factor and factor != n and factor != 1:\n                return [factor], n // factor\n        \n        # If Pollard Rho fails after multiple retries, return the original number,\n        # implying this strategy could not find a factor.\n        return [], n\n\n    def _pollard_rho(self, n: int) -> Optional[int]:\n        \"\"\"Performs the core Pollard's Rho algorithm to find a single factor.\"\"\"\n        if n % 2 == 0:\n            return 2 # Handle even numbers, though not expected as input to this method\n        if n <= 3:\n            return None # Pollard Rho not suitable for very small numbers\n\n        # Seed random for deterministic behavior if set\n        if self.seed is not None:\n            random.seed(self.seed)\n\n        x = random.randint(2, n - 1)\n        y = x\n        c = random.randint(1, n - 1)\n        \n        d = 1 # GCD\n        \n        for _ in range(self.MAX_ITERATIONS_PER_ATTEMPT):\n            x = (x * x + c) % n\n            y = (y * y + c) % n\n            y = (y * y + c) % n # Tortoise and Hare; y moves twice as fast\n\n            d = math.gcd(abs(x - y), n)\n\n            if d == n:\n                # Algorithm got stuck or n is prime; try with new random parameters\n                x = random.randint(2, n - 1)\n                y = x\n                c = random.randint(1, n - 1)\n                d = 1\n                continue # Restart loop with new parameters\n\n            if d != 1: # Found a non-trivial factor\n                return d\n        \n        return None # Failed to find a factor after max iterations\n```\n\n```python\n# src/primefactor/factorizer.py\nimport sys\nimport time\nfrom typing import List, Optional, Tuple\nfrom .cache import FactorCache\nfrom .repository import FactorRepository\nfrom .strategy_base import FactorizationStrategy\nfrom .primes import PrimeSieve\nfrom .exceptions import InvalidInputError, FactorizationError\n\nclass Factorizer:\n    \"\"\"Orchestrates factorization flow, handles input validation, caching, and strategy selection.\"\"\"\n    DEFAULT_SMALL_BOUND = 10_000\n\n    def __init__(self, cache: FactorCache, repo: Optional[FactorRepository], \n                 strategies: List[FactorizationStrategy], sieve: PrimeSieve, \n                 small_bound: int = DEFAULT_SMALL_BOUND) -> None:\n        self.cache = cache\n        self.repo = repo\n        self.strategies = strategies\n        self.sieve = sieve # Used for primality checks within factorizer\n        self.small_bound = small_bound\n\n    def _factor_recursive(self, n_val: int) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"Recursively applies factorization strategies to break down a number.\"\"\"\n        if n_val == 1:\n            return []\n        if self.sieve.is_prime(n_val):\n            return [n_val]\n\n        # Try each strategy in order\n        for strategy in self.strategies:\n            try:\n                found_factors, remaining_n = strategy.factorize(n_val)\n                \n                # If the strategy found any factors or successfully reduced n_val\n                if found_factors or remaining_n < n_val:\n                    # Recursively factor the remaining part\n                    recursive_factors = self._factor_recursive(remaining_n)\n                    # Combine and return sorted factors\n                    return sorted(found_factors + recursive_factors)\n            except FactorizationError as e:\n                # Log strategy specific error and continue with next strategy or re-raise\n                print(f\"Warning: Strategy {type(strategy).__name__} failed for {n_val}: {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n                # If a strategy throws, the orchestrator should decide whether to fall back or fail\n                # For now, it will try the next strategy. If all fail, the loop finishes.\n        \n        # If no strategy could factorize the number, this indicates a failure.\n        # This should ideally not be reached for valid composite numbers.\n        raise FactorizationError(f\"Failed to factorize {n_val} completely after trying all strategies.\")\n\n    def factorize(self, n: int) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"Public API to factorize an integer, returning a sorted list of prime factors.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(n, int) or n < 2:\n            raise InvalidInputError(\"n must be an integer greater than or equal to 2.\")\n        \n        result_metadata = self.factorize_with_metadata(n)\n        return result_metadata[\"factors\"]\n\n    def factorize_with_metadata(self, n: int) -> dict:\n        \"\"\"Factorizes an integer and returns factors along with metadata (from_cache, duration_ms).\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(n, int) or n < 2:\n            raise InvalidInputError(\"n must be an integer greater than or equal to 2.\")\n\n        start_time = time.perf_counter()\n        from_cache = False\n        factors = None\n\n        # 1. Try in-memory cache\n        factors = self.cache.get(n)\n        if factors:\n            from_cache = True\n        else:\n            # 2. Try persistent cache (if configured and available)\n            if self.repo:\n                try:\n                    factors = self.repo.get(n)\n                    if factors:\n                        self.cache.set(n, factors) # Populate in-memory cache after retrieving from DB\n                        from_cache = True\n                except Exception as e: # Catch any DB-related error gracefully\n                    print(f\"Warning: Persistent cache read failed for {n}: {e}. Disabling persistence for this session.\", file=sys.stderr)\n                    self.repo = None # Disable repo to prevent further issues\n\n        if not factors:\n            # 3. Compute factorization using strategies if not found in any cache\n            factors = self._factor_recursive(n)\n            \n            # Post-computation: store in caches\n            self.cache.set(n, factors)\n            if self.repo:\n                try:\n                    self.repo.set(n, factors)\n                except Exception as e: # Catch any DB-related error gracefully\n                    print(f\"Warning: Persistent cache write failed for {n}: {e}. Disabling persistence for this session.\", file=sys.stderr)\n                    self.repo = None\n\n        duration_ms = (time.perf_counter() - start_time) * 1000.0\n        return {\"n\": n, \"factors\": factors, \"from_cache\": from_cache, \"duration_ms\": duration_ms}\n```\n\n```python\n# src/primefactor/api.py\nfrom .cache import FactorCache\nfrom .repository import FactorRepository\nfrom .primes import PrimeSieve\nfrom .trial_division import TrialDivisionStrategy\nfrom .pollard_rho import PollardRhoStrategy\nfrom .factorizer import Factorizer\n\n# Global instances for the API to share resources\n_sieve = PrimeSieve()\n_cache = FactorCache()\n_repo = FactorRepository() # Initialize repository, it handles schema creation\n_strategies = [TrialDivisionStrategy(_sieve), PollardRhoStrategy()]\n_factorizer_instance = Factorizer(cache=_cache, repo=_repo, strategies=_strategies, sieve=_sieve)\n\ndef factorize_api(n: int) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"\n    Programmatic API wrapper to factorize an integer and return structured metadata.\n    Returns: {'n': int, 'factors': List[int], 'from_cache': bool, 'duration_ms': float}\n    \"\"\"\n    return _factorizer_instance.factorize_with_metadata(n)\n```\n\n```python\n# src/primefactor/cli.py\nimport sys\nimport json\nimport argparse\nfrom typing import List\nfrom .api import factorize_api\nfrom .exceptions import InvalidInputError, FactorizationError\n\ndef build_cli() -> argparse.ArgumentParser:\n    \"\"\"Builds the ArgumentParser for the CLI.\"\"\"\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n        prog=\"primefactor\",\n        description=\"Compute prime factorization of an integer.\"\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"n\",\n        type=int,\n        help=\"The integer to factorize (must be >= 2).\"\n    )\n    # Future arguments could be added here for cache config, DB path, etc.\n    return parser\n\ndef main(argv: Optional[List[str]] = None) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Main entry point for the command-line interface.\"\"\"\n    if argv is None:\n        argv = sys.argv[1:]\n\n    parser = build_cli()\n    args = parser.parse_args(argv)\n\n    try:\n        res = factorize_api(args.n)\n        print(json.dumps(res, indent=2)) # Pretty print JSON\n        return 0\n    except InvalidInputError as e:\n        print(f\"Error: Invalid input: {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return 1\n    except FactorizationError as e:\n        print(f\"Error: Factorization failed: {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return 2\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(f\"An unexpected error occurred: {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return 3\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    sys.exit(main())\n```\n\n```python\n# src/primefactor/__init__.py\nfrom .api import factorize_api\nfrom .factorizer import Factorizer\nfrom .cache import FactorCache\nfrom .repository import FactorRepository\nfrom .primes import PrimeSieve\nfrom .trial_division import TrialDivisionStrategy\nfrom .pollard_rho import PollardRhoStrategy\nfrom typing import List\n\n__version__ = \"0.1.0\"\n\n# Global instances for the top-level convenience function\n# These are separate from the API's global instances if one wants to use Factorizer\n# directly with different configurations, but for the convenience function,\n# it's usually sufficient to mirror the API's default setup.\n_sieve_global = PrimeSieve()\n_cache_global = FactorCache()\n_repo_global = FactorRepository() # Persistent cache, schema created at init\n_strategies_global = [TrialDivisionStrategy(_sieve_global), PollardRhoStrategy()]\n_factorizer_global = Factorizer(cache=_cache_global, repo=_repo_global, strategies=_strategies_global, sieve=_sieve_global)\n\ndef factorize(n: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Return a list of prime factors of the given integer in ascending order,\n    including multiplicity.\n\n    >>> factorize(8)\n    [2, 2, 2]\n    >>> factorize(25)\n    [5, 5]\n    >>> factorize(70)\n    [2, 5, 7]\n    \"\"\"\n    return _factorizer_global.factorize(n)\n```"}
